{{also|Cadence|cadencé}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=cadence}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|cadence}}, from {{der|en|it|cadenza}}, from {{der|en|la|*cadentia}}, form of {{m|la|cadēns}}, form of {{m|la|cadō|t=I fall, I cease}}, from {{der|en|itc-pro|*kadō}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ḱad-|t=to fall}}. {{doublet|cadenza|lang=en}} and {{m|en|chance}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈkeɪ.dn̩s/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act or state of declining or sinking.
#* Milton
#*: Now was the sun in western '''cadence''' low.
# Balanced, rhythmic flow.
#* Shakespeare
#*: golden '''cadence''' of poesy
#* {{quote-journal|date=2 December 1991|title=At the Saudi-Kuwaiti Border|work=ABC Nightline|passage=Night has now passed in the Saudi desert and as we hear from Nightline correspondent Forrest Sawyer, the normal '''cadence''' of life at the front is about to change.}}
# The measure or beat of movement.
#* {{quote-journal|year=1993|volume=98|issue=5|page=102|title=Terror of the deep|author=Ken Schultz|journal=Field and Stream|passage=Getting into a good jigging rhythm means making short quick jerks in a regular '''cadence''' that might average about one jerk every 1.5 to 2 seconds.}}
# The general inflection or modulation of the voice, or of any sound.
#* Milton
#*: Blustering winds, which all night long / Had roused the sea, now with hoarse '''cadence''' lull / Seafaring men o'erwatched.
#* Sir Walter Scott
#*: The accents {{...}} were in passion's tenderest '''cadence'''.
#* {{quote-book
|year=1986
|author={{w|John le Carré}}
|title={{w|A Perfect Spy}}
|section=
|passage= Then away at last they sped to the house or bedside of some elderly and worthy person, and Pym sat fascinated to see how swiftly Rick trimmed his manner to suit theirs, how naturally he slipped into the '''cadences''' and vernacular that put them most at ease, and how the love of God came into his good face when he talked about Liberalism and Masonry and his dear dead father, God rest him, and a firstclass rate of return, ten percent guaranteed plus profits for as long as you're spared.}}
#* {{quote-journal|date=30 December 1991|title=Raimey: A Breath of Fresh Ayah|author=David Holmstrom|work=Christian Science Monitor|passage=The '''cadence''' of Raimey's voice is pure Down-Easter Maine}}
# {{lb|en|music}} A progression of at least two [[chord|chords]] which conclude a piece of [[music]], section or musical phrases within it. Sometimes referred to [[analogy|analogously]] as musical [[punctuation]].
# {{lb|en|music}} A [[cadenza]], or closing embellishment; a pause before the end of a [[strain]], which the performer may fill with a [[flight of fancy]].
# {{lb|en|speech}} A fall in [[inflection]] of a speaker’s voice, such as at the end of a sentence.
# {{lb|en|dance}} A dance move which ends a phrase.
#: ''The '''cadence''' in a galliard step refers to the final leap in a cinquepace sequence.''
# {{lb|en|fencing}} The rhythm and sequence of a series of actions.
# {{lb|en|running}} The number of steps per minute.
# {{lb|en|cycling}} The number of revolutions per minute of the [[crank]]s or pedals of a [[bicycle]].
# {{lb|en|military}} A chant that is sung by military personnel while running or marching; a [[jody call]].
# {{lb|en|heraldry}} [[cadency]]
# {{lb|en|horse-riding}} Harmony and proportion of movement, as in a well-managed [[horse]].

